# Jumperless V5: routable nodes vs pad-only nodes

Ground truth is `rev5plusXmap` in `src/MatrixState.cpp` (chips I/J/K/L x-pin
rows): a node is routable ONLY if it appears there (or is a breadboard row on
chips A-H). Everything else named in `src/JumperlessDefines.h` is a probe-pad
position, an LED position, or a hardwired signal - `connect()` /
`addBridgeToState()` on it does nothing.

## Crossbar-routable

- Breadboard rows `1`-`60`
- Nano header signals: `NANO_D0`-`NANO_D13`, `NANO_A0`-`NANO_A7`, `NANO_AREF`
- `DAC0`, `DAC1`
- `ADC0`-`ADC3`, `ADC4` (`ADC4_5V`, 5V-tolerant divider)
- `TOP_RAIL`, `BOTTOM_RAIL`, `GND`
- `ISENSE_PLUS` / `ISENSE_MINUS` (INA0 shunt; took the V5 crosspoints of the
  old `NANO_RESET_0/1`)
- `RP_GPIO_1`-`RP_GPIO_8` (nodes 131-138, RP2350 pins 20-27)
- `RP_UART_TX` / `RP_UART_RX` (= `RP_GPIO_16/17`)
- `ROUTABLE_BUFFER_IN` / `ROUTABLE_BUFFER_OUT` (probe measure buffer)

## NOT routable

- `NANO_3V3`, `NANO_5V`, `NANO_VIN`, `NANO_GND_0/1`, `NANO_RESET_0/1`,
  `NANO_RESET` - probe-sense pad positions on the Nano header only. There is
  NO routable fixed 3.3V or 5V rail; the stiffest routable 3.3V source is a
  DAC set to 3.3V (or a GPIO driven high, which droops ~41 ohm-ish under
  load).
- `LOGO_PAD_TOP/BOTTOM`, `GPIO_PAD`, `DAC_PAD`, `ADC_PAD`,
  `BUILDING_PAD_TOP/BOTTOM` - capacitive/logo probe pads.
- `SUPPLY_3V3`, `SUPPLY_5V`, `SUPPLY_8V_P/N` - LED/legacy positions
  (8V ones "not actually connected to anything").
- `ADC5` (probe tip sense divider), `ADC7` (hardwired to buffer output =
  probe tip in measure mode), `ADC6` (power monitor) - fixed wiring, read
  them but don't route them.
- `RP_GPIO_18/19` - dedicated Arduino reset lines, not crosspoints.
